 \n  `glPosition` requires a `vec4` because it represents a point in homogeneous coordinates, allowing for transformat... \n                                                                                                                       \n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────                                                           \n> Why does glPosition require a vec4 not vec3?                                                                         \n                                                                                                                       \n▸ Thought for 5s, 389 tokens                                                                                           \n  `glPosition` requires a `vec4` because it represents a point in homogeneous coordinates, allowing for transformat... \n  gl_Position requires a vec4 because modern GPU hardware uses homogeneous coordinates (x, y, z, w) for clipping and   \n  perspective projection.                                                                                              \n                                                                                                                       \n  The 4th component w is used by the GPU hardware to perform the perspective divide:                                   \n                                                                                                                       \n    NDC = (x / w,  y / w,  z / w)                                                                                      \n                                                                                                                       \n  In 3D cameras, the projection matrix places the camera depth into w. Dividing by w scales geometry so that distant   \n  objects shrink and near objects enlarge.                                                                             \n                                                             